SLIDES = [
 dict(n=1, title="Keeping a first SIP workable when cash flow changes",
  core="The goal is more first-time investors still active at month 12, and the question I chased is what happens when someone wants to keep investing but their cash flow doesn't cooperate.",
  notes="Starting a SIP takes a few taps. Keeping it going for a year is harder. Groww's goal here is simple to state: of the people who start their first SIP, how many still have a successful instalment in the 30 days around month 12? That's the North Star on the right, and I kept the assignment's definition exactly.\n\nThe journey across the bottom is how I think about that year: you choose a date, you get ready for each debit, the debit succeeds or doesn't, and then you either adjust or drift. My hypothesis, and I'll be careful to call it that, is that a lot of first SIPs don't end with a decision to stop. They end because the date and the money stopped lining up, and the way back wasn't clear.\n\nI built two things to test that: a discovery engine over public conversations, and a SIP Companion that explains an investor's own SIP and their options. Both are live, and the links are on the slide.",
  evidence="North Star and active-SIP definition from the assignment. Hypothesis synthesised from slides 6–7 (5 interviews, n = 20 survey, 352 coded public records).",
  transition="To know where to intervene, I first broke the North Star into the moments that decide it.",
  challenge="Why month-12 continuation and not number of instalments paid or AUM?",
  answer="Because it's the company's stated goal and it measures whether the habit survived the first year. A '10 of 12 paid' metric would punish people who skipped a month on purpose, and AUM is driven by amount and markets. I treat instalment-level metrics as diagnostics, not the target."),
 dict(n=2, title="Four moments in year one decide month-12 continuation",
  core="Continuation is won or lost at four moments, and only month 12 is the North Star; the rest are diagnostics I'm proposing, not measuring.",
  notes="I broke the year into four moments. At the start, someone picks an amount and a date. Before each debit, the money has to be there. Then the instalment either goes through, fails as an auto-debit, or gets skipped on purpose. And after any interruption, the investor pays it, adjusts, pauses or stops.\n\nUnder each moment there's a leading indicator Groww could track. I want to be clear: these are proposals. This was an independent study, so none of them are measured Groww metrics.\n\nThe strip at the bottom matters to me. A missed instalment isn't churn, and stopping a SIP isn't redeeming your units. If you pool all of these as 'SIP failure', you design the wrong fix.",
  evidence="Survey 10/20 matched date to payday; interviews 4/5 timed debits or checked balance; public 52/352 payment failure; survey 4/20 failed (3 waited, 1 paused, 0 paid); interviews 2/5 failed or error, 1/5 skipped on purpose; survey 9/20 skipped or paused on purpose, 8 restarted.",
  transition="To find which of these moments matters most, I started with what investors say in public.",
  challenge="Are any of these leading indicators actually correlated with month-12 continuation?",
  answer="I don't know yet; I don't have Groww's cohort data. They're chosen because each sits on the causal path I'm hypothesising. The first thing I'd do with internal data is check which of them predicts month-12 status before optimising any of them."),
 dict(n=3, title="From 19,005 public records to 450 coded SIP experiences",
  core="The engine turned 19,005 public records into 450 AI-coded SIP experiences, and I'm explicit that it finds mechanisms, not prevalence.",
  notes="I collected 19,005 public records in October: Reddit, TradingQ&A, YouTube comments, and Play Store and App Store reviews. Keyword gates kept 2,518 that were about a SIP and written in the first person. Claude coded 450 of them against a written codebook, which went through three versions.\n\nBefore anyone uses these numbers, the panel on the right is the honest part. I didn't document how the 450 were picked from the 2,518, so it can't be reproduced. Rejected records weren't kept. Reddit is 62% of everything collected. And 78% of the coded set is lapse-labelled because I went looking for SIP problems, so that's not a lapse rate.\n\nI checked the AI against my own blind labels in three rounds of 20. Agreement went from slight to moderate, kappa 0.15 to 0.50, 11 of 20 in the last round. That's good enough to find patterns, not good enough to trust any single label.",
  evidence="Dataset counts: 19,005 / 2,518 / 450 (352 lapse, 50 continuer, 48 context); 409 AI first-hand; 60 human-checked; κ 0.15, 0.29, 0.50; raw 4/20, 7/20, 11/20; 16,487 filtered out and 2,068 uncoded, neither retained; Reddit 11,802 of 19,005.",
  transition="So what did those 352 lapse stories actually say?",
  challenge="How did you pick the 450, and why should I trust anything downstream of an undocumented sample?",
  answer="Reddit's 1,553 matches were sampled down to 280 candidates; beyond that I didn't record the rule, and I say so on the slide and the methodology page. That's why I use the engine to surface mechanisms and to rank where to look, then test those mechanisms in interviews. I never use it to size the problem."),
 dict(n=4, title="Trust is discussed most; payment failure is close behind",
  core="Platform trust is the loudest complaint, but payment failure is close behind and much closer to the moment a SIP ends.",
  notes="Each lapse story gets one primary driver, so the eight bars add up to 352. Platform trust tops the list: allotment delays, statement mismatches, redemption waits. Payment failure is fourth with 52.\n\nOne caveat I want to flag myself: when a story fits several drivers, the codebook gives it to the one closest to an exit, and payment failure wins those ties. That can only push its count up, so I'd rather say it than have you find it.\n\nThe quotes are verbatim from the records, and each ID opens in the Evidence Explorer.",
  evidence="TRUST 64, EXPECT 58, STOP 57, PAY 52, CONFUSE 40, AMOUNT 38, PAUSE 33, OUTSIDE 10 = 352. Quotes A27, R40, P18, A19. Tie rule PAY > STOP > PAUSE > AMOUNT > EXPECT.",
  transition="Volume alone doesn't tell me where a product can help, so I scored each driver.",
  challenge="Isn't the tie rule just rigging the result for payment failure?",
  answer="It could inflate it, which is why it's disclosed. Even so, payment failure is fourth by raw volume; its rank on the next slide comes from the weights, not the count. And 22 labels moved out of payment failure when the codebook tightened, so I've been pruning it, not padding it."),
 dict(n=5, title="Payment failure ranks first on addressability, not volume",
  core="Payment failure ranks first because it's close to an exit and fixable in the app, and I can show exactly how much that depends on my own weights.",
  notes="The score is share times how directly a driver ends a SIP, times how much the app can do about it, times evidence quality. Trust has more stories, but trust problems rarely end a SIP on their own and they mostly sit in operations: registrars, banks, allotment. Payment failure is the opposite: three failed instalments in a row cancel a monthly SIP, and the status, reason and retry all live in the app.\n\nThe disclosure I'd point you to is at the bottom right. The ranking survives resampling: 945 of 1,000 bootstrap runs and 937 of 1,000 weight nudges keep payment failure first. But if you remove my weights entirely, it drops to fourth. So this is a judgement call I'm owning, not a finding the data forced on me.",
  evidence="Scores 13.3, 9.7, 8.1, 7.8, 7.5, 2.6, 2.2, 0.4; PAY 14.8 × 1.0 × 0.9 × 1.00; TRUST 18.2 × 0.5 × 0.3 × 0.97; stability 945/1,000 (seed 20261023), 937/1,000 (±0.15, seed 42); unweighted rank 4.",
  transition="A ranking is a hypothesis, so I took it to real investors.",
  challenge="If removing the weights drops it to fourth, isn't the ranking just your opinion?",
  answer="The weights are opinions, written down with reasons and open to change on the Opportunity Ranking page. What I'm claiming is narrower: given an explicit view of exit relevance and addressability, payment failure is where to look first. The interviews on the next two slides are where I tested that, and they changed my framing."),
 dict(n=6, title="Interviewees plan around cash flow; few hit failed debits",
  core="The interviews showed more people managing cash timing than recovering from failures, and only one of five is a confirmed Groww first-SIP investor.",
  notes="I spoke to five people and ran a separate survey of 20. I keep them apart and never add their counts together.\n\nWhat surprised me was how much planning happens before anything breaks. Four of the five timed their debit to when money arrives or checked their balance first. Only two had a failed debit or an error, P03 and P04. P05 missed contributions on purpose when pocket money was short. That isn't a failed auto-debit, and I've stopped calling it one.\n\nI also want to be upfront about eligibility. Only P03 is confirmed as a first SIP on Groww. P02 uses Zerodha Coin, P01's and P04's first SIPs weren't on Groww, and P05's platform is unclear. And P03 has since stopped the SIP. So the segment on the right is provisional.",
  evidence="Workbook plus corrections of 5 Nov. Debit timing or balance 4/5 (P01–P04); money limits 4/5 (P02–P05); looked outside 4/5 (P01–P04); failed or error 2/5 (P03, P04); missed on purpose 1/5 (P05); first SIP on Groww 1/5 (P03); active 4/5. Survey: 4/20 failed, 10/20 payday, 9/20 skipped or paused, 8/20 on Groww.",
  transition="Putting the public evidence and the interviews side by side changed how I defined the problem.",
  challenge="Only one of five is a Groww first-SIP investor. How can you call this research on your segment?",
  answer="It's a fair hit. Recruitment through colleagues gave me first-year investors, but mostly not on Groww. I use the interviews for behaviour, which looks similar across platforms, not for Groww-specific claims, and I label the segment provisional. Recruiting eligible Groww first-SIP investors is the first thing I'd fix with more time."),
 dict(n=7, title="Interviews widened the problem beyond failed payments",
  core="This is where my thinking changed: the engine pointed at failed payments, the interviews pointed at cash timing, so the problem became cash-flow fit plus clarity about options.",
  notes="This is the slide I'd most like you to remember. The engine ranked payment failure first. If I'd followed it, I'd have built a better failed-payment screen.\n\nThe interviews challenged that. Only two of five had a failure; four of five were quietly managing when cash arrives. P03, the one confirmed Groww investor, did have two low-balance failures, but what hurt was not knowing the rules: how many misses cancel a SIP, whether invested money was affected. P05 skipped and assumed the SIP would just carry on.\n\nSo I widened the problem. It's not only recovering a failed debit. It's helping someone understand an interruption, changing cash and the options they actually have. I'm stating it as a hypothesis, because five interviews can't prove a pattern, but it's the one the evidence supports best.",
  evidence="Engine PAY 13.3 (slide 5). Interviews 2/5 failure or error vs 4/5 timing or balance. Pathways: P04 Q08, P05 Q10–Q14, P03 Q08–Q16; survey 10/20, 4/20, 9/20; public 52/352, 57/352.",
  transition="Here's what I built for that problem.",
  challenge="Isn't 'cash-flow fit' just a fancier name for 'people are short of money', which a product can't fix?",
  answer="A product can't create money, agreed. What it can do is reduce the gap between what people intend and what happens: debit dates that match income, a clear view of what one miss means versus three, and skip or lower options that are as visible as Pay. Those are the levers in the Companion and the roadmap."),
 dict(n=8, title="Companion explains your situation, then your options",
  core="The Companion explains the investor's own SIP and the options the app actually allows, with code deciding eligibility and AI only explaining.",
  notes="Three screens tell the story. First, the investor sees their SIP: two of three misses, reason confirmed. Second, they ask in their own words and the answer uses their dates, their amount and the actual rules. Third, they pick an option and review it before anything happens, and it's clearly marked as a simulation.\n\nI chose AI here because people don't ask FAQ-shaped questions. 'Can I stop without paying?' has a different answer for every SIP, depending on when the next debit is. A fixed FAQ can't combine the rule with your situation. But the model never decides what's allowed. Code computes eligibility, the model drafts from 22 reviewed facts, and 15 checks run before anything is shown. If the model is unavailable or a draft fails a check, a rule-based reply is shown and labelled as such.\n\nThe screenshots are from the production code, run in rule-based mode, and they're labelled that way. There's no Groww account integration, and no money moves.",
  evidence="Production build 231973d; 22 facts, 13 sources; 15 checks; ICE 20.0 / 6.7 built, 10.0 / 7.5 next, 4.5 later (23 Oct, pre-interview).",
  transition="Building it is one thing; the question is how much of it I can vouch for.",
  challenge="Why build an AI Companion instead of a deterministic FAQ?",
  answer="The rule engine already answers the common questions, and it's the fallback. AI earns its place on the long tail: questions phrased in Hinglish, follow-ups like 'and if I skip that one too?', and combinations of state and rule. The checks and the fallback mean the AI can make answers better without being able to make them unsafe."),
 dict(n=9, title="Safeguards tested; retention impact not yet established",
  core="I can show how the AI behaves and how accurate its claims are, but user value rests on one participant's feedback so far.",
  notes="I keep three kinds of evidence separate.\n\nFirst, technical evaluation. On 25 locked cases the live product passed 22, and 15 of 16 live AI answers passed. Since then I've inspected those cases, so I now treat them as regression cases. The rule engine alone passed 20 of 25. The 107 out of 107 is on a set I tuned against, so it isn't independent. A post-fix live run on the final build is pending, and it's marked that way.\n\nSecond, I read 58 individual claims by hand: 57 were correct. The one wrong one told a user they had to pay before cancelling when they didn't, and that's now blocked by a check.\n\nThird, users. P05 used it and said it clarifies options and helps people decide. That's one person, it's qualitative, and it's not a usability score. I didn't run the formal task protocol, and I haven't yet collected P03's and P04's feedback, so this falls short of the three-user ask.",
  evidence="Companion v3.2 report: 22/25 (201ceaa, live product), 15/16 live, 20/25 rule-based (2000f15), 16/16 red team, 24/24 dialogue turns, 107/107 tuned; claim audit 57/58, 53/58, 56/58, 14/15; engine 26/26 tuned, 7/10 held-out; P05 quote.",
  transition="Given those gaps, the last question is how this could fail and what I'd do next.",
  challenge="How do you know the Companion is useful to real users?",
  answer="I don't, beyond one participant's qualitative feedback. What I can show is that it behaves safely and gets its facts right on the cases I tested. Usefulness needs sessions with eligible Groww first-SIP investors on a real task, and then an experiment on the leading indicators. That's step 2 and 3 of my plan."),
 dict(n=10, title="Guardrails keep it explanatory: no fund picks, no pressure",
  core="The guardrails keep the Companion explanatory, and the next steps are about proving value, not adding features.",
  notes="The risks I worry about most are wrong facts, nudging people to pay when they shouldn't, and offering an option that isn't really available. Each has a guardrail in the MVP today, and the right column says what I'd monitor or do next.\n\nTwo honest points. The model runs on a free tier, so under load you'll see the rule-based fallback, and the app says so. And the research is Reddit-heavy, with only one confirmed Groww participant, so the next steps start with validation, not features.\n\nI'll end where the product started: help investors understand their SIP situation and their options, without pressure and without telling them what to invest in.",
  evidence="Guardrails present in production build 231973d; facts verified 26 Oct–5 Nov; next steps are proposals.",
  transition="(Close.) Happy to go to the live Companion or any part of the evidence.",
  challenge="What would a real Groww rollout need that this prototype doesn't have?",
  answer="Real SIP status from the system of record, a policy and legal review of every fact and option, a privacy review before any personal data is used, model capacity beyond a free tier, and a controlled experiment measuring the leading indicators and, over time, month-12 continuation."),
]

QA = [
 ("Why month-12 active SIP continuation as the North Star?",
  "It's the stated company goal, and it captures whether the habit survived the first year. 'At least one successful instalment in the last 30 days' is generous on purpose: it counts someone who skipped a month and came back. I use instalment-level metrics only as diagnostics.",
  "Whether month 12 is the right horizon for Groww's economics; I haven't seen cohort curves."),
 ("Why public online conversations?",
  "They're the fastest way to find mechanisms in investors' own words, at scale, without accessing anyone's account. I used them to generate and rank hypotheses, then tested those in interviews.",
  "Public posts over-represent complaints and Reddit users, so they can't size anything."),
 ("How did you select the final 450 records?",
  "From 2,518 that passed a topic gate and a first-person gate. Reddit's 1,553 matches were sampled down to 280 candidates. Beyond that, the selection rule wasn't recorded, and it's disclosed as not reproducible.",
  "The exact selection, and the 16,487 and 2,068 records that weren't retained."),
 ("Is 78.2% the actual SIP lapse rate?",
  "No. 352 of 450 coded records are lapse-labelled because the filters targeted SIP problems. It describes the sample, not investors.",
  "The real lapse rate; that needs Groww cohort data."),
 ("How reliable is a Cohen's kappa of 0.50?",
  "Moderate, and preliminary: 11 of 20 in round 3, up from 4/20 and 7/20. Enough to trust patterns across dozens of records, not any single label.",
  "First-hand status (409/450) was never human-checked; 38 disagreements were reconciled by one reviewer, none independently."),
 ("Why is payment failure prioritised over platform trust?",
  "Trust has more stories (64 vs 52) but trust issues rarely end a SIP on their own and sit mostly in operations. A failed instalment counts towards automatic cancellation after three in a row, and its status, reason and retry live in the app. So exit 1.0 vs 0.5, addressability 0.9 vs 0.3.",
  "The weights are analyst judgements."),
 ("Why does payment failure rank fourth when you remove the weights?",
  "Because by raw volume it is fourth. The first-place ranking depends on my view of exit relevance and addressability, which is why I disclose it. It survives noise (945/1,000 resamples, 937/1,000 weight nudges) but not removing the weights.",
  "Whether my weights match Groww's internal data on which issues actually precede a stop."),
 ("How did the primary interviews challenge your original conclusion?",
  "The engine pointed at failed payments. In interviews only 2/5 had a failed debit or error, while 4/5 planned around when cash arrives, and P05's misses were intentional. So I widened the problem to cash-flow fit and clarity of options.",
  "Five interviews can't establish how common each pattern is."),
 ("Why cash-flow friction as the problem to solve?",
  "It shows up in every source: the timing and balance behaviour in 4/5 interviews, 10/20 survey respondents aligning the date to payday, 9/20 skipping or pausing on purpose, and amount-vs-cash-flow and skip/pause stories in the public data. It's also addressable in the app: dates, amounts, skips and explanations.",
  "Whether solving it moves month-12 continuation."),
 ("Why an AI Companion instead of a deterministic FAQ?",
  "The answer depends on the investor's own state, like when the next debit is, and people ask in their own words and follow up. The rule engine covers the common paths and is the fallback; AI handles phrasing and combinations. Code, not the model, decides what's allowed.",
  "Whether users prefer the conversational format to a well-designed status screen; untested."),
 ("How do you stop an LLM from inventing Groww policies?",
  "The model only sees 22 reviewed facts from 13 sources and the SIP's state. Every draft passes 15 deterministic checks, covering grounding, numbers, advice, urgency, pressure to pay and unavailable actions, or it's replaced by a checked rule-based answer. A human audit of 58 claims found 57 correct; the one error is now blocked.",
  "Paraphrases the checks miss; scoring is regex-based."),
 ("What happens when Groq is unavailable?",
  "The rule-based engine answers with the same checks, and the reply is labelled, for example 'Rule-based · AI limit reached'. It never shows a fallback as live AI.",
  "Live quality under real load; the free tier allows roughly 130–140 live answers a day per model."),
 ("How do you know the Companion is useful to actual users?",
  "Today, only from P05's qualitative feedback. I haven't run task-based tests and haven't collected P03's and P04's feedback, which is below the assignment's three-user requirement.",
  "Usefulness for eligible Groww first-SIP investors."),
 ("Why haven't you shown actual retention improvement?",
  "Month-12 continuation takes a year and requires Groww's cohort data and a controlled rollout. I've defined the North Star and leading indicators so that an experiment could measure it, but I won't claim an effect I haven't measured.",
  "Everything about impact."),
 ("What would you do with another four weeks?",
  "First, recruit six to eight eligible Groww first-SIP investors and run real-task sessions. Second, run the controlled live evaluation on the final build. Third, prototype the payday-aware date and pre-debit readiness. Fourth, design the experiment with guardrails.",
  "—"),
 ("Only one of five interviewees is a confirmed Groww first-SIP investor.",
  "Correct, and it's on the slide. I use the interviews for behaviour, not Groww-specific claims, and the segment is labelled provisional.",
  "Groww-specific friction points."),
 ("The survey is small.",
  "20 starters from a convenience sample on mixed platforms, 8 on Groww. I report it as x of 20 and never mix it with interview counts.",
  "Anything about prevalence."),
 ("AI-classified first-hand records?",
  "409 of 450 were labelled first-hand by the AI and never human-checked. That's why first-hand only feeds the evidence-quality term, and why the disclosure is on slide 3.",
  "First-hand label accuracy."),
 ("No formal task-based usability study?",
  "Right. The pre-registered T1–T5 protocol wasn't feasible in time. I show only the qualitative feedback that actually exists.",
  "Task success and time on task."),
 ("No Groww account integration?",
  "Deliberate. The Companion uses illustrative scenarios, and every action is a labelled simulation. A real version would read status from Groww's system of record.",
  "Integration constraints."),
 ("Real rollout and regulatory dependencies?",
  "Every fact needs policy and compliance sign-off, there can be no advice or return claims (SEBI expectations), a privacy review is needed before personal data is used, and model capacity is needed beyond a free tier.",
  "Groww's internal review requirements."),
]

DEMO_LIVE = [
 "Open nl-groww-sip.vercel.app/companion. Scenario A loads: ₹3,000 monthly SIP, 'Needs attention', 2 of 3 missed in a row, reason: insufficient balance (confirmed). Say: 'This is an illustrative first SIP with two misses in a row.'",
 "Tap 'Why did my last instalment fail?'. Point to the answer using this SIP's dates, the 3-in-a-row rule and the Pay Now window. Point to 'Checked 15/15' and the mode label under the reply.",
 "Read the label aloud: if it says 'Live AI', say so. If it says 'Rule-based · …', say: 'The live model is rate-limited right now, so this is the checked rule-based answer.'",
 "Type: 'Money is tight this month. What are my options?' Show that skip, lower, change date and stop are listed neutrally, and nobody is pushed to pay.",
 "Tap 'Skip next'. On the review sheet show what changes, what stays the same and the rule, then the note 'Simulation: confirming changes this demo only. No money moves and no real SIP changes.' Tap Cancel or confirm.",
 "Close: 'Code decided which options exist; the AI only explained them.'",
]
DEMO_FALLBACK_NOTE = "If the label shows 'Rule-based · AI limit reached' or 'AI unavailable', run the same steps. Say once: 'Live AI is on a free tier and is rate-limited right now; this is the rule-based engine with the same 15 checks.' Never call a rule-based reply live AI."
DEMO_EMERGENCY = "If the site doesn't load: go to slide 8. 'Here's the same flow as screenshots from the production code: the SIP card with two misses, the answer that applies the rules to this SIP, and the review sheet that marks the skip as a simulation. Code decides eligibility, the model drafts from reviewed facts, and 15 checks run before anything is shown. The link is in the deck if you'd like to try it afterwards.'"

